# L §2.4 (tally 2.1.3, issue #59): a row that leaves a trailing key out sorts before one that states `*` there, as "a
# keyless row first" puts no key before every key, `*` included; keys decide before spans, so GD-28's line order isn't
# reached. x[e1] = 0 (from 2027) prints before x[e1, *] = 1 (to 2026), though by span (a span with no start first) or
# by line (`,` before `]`) the other would be first. (L §4.6: the two rows are not "at the same keys" and neither is
# narrower; their spans don't overlap, so both stand.) Was a crash, as L2_4_reprint_key_left_out.
=== options reprint
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2027-12-31

source s "s"

index ex = [e1, e2]
index item = [savings, shares]
input x[e in ex, i in item]: int

version v
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  x[e1, *] = 1 governs ..2026
  x[e1] = 0 governs 2027..
